From 67b9ba5e5cf8d7350bb47057f0e13dcf5ef1b063 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Freddie Honohan <honohanf@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 21:57:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] Spend: shifts, splices, truncated pushes and the
 unlock/lock boundary as ts-sdk Spend has them

- OP_LSHIFTNUM / OP_RSHIFTNUM: a negative or > INT_MAX bit count is an error (it was read as 0); a left shift
  past the memory limit is refused before it is allocated (a 39 GB allocation aborted the process).
- OP_SUBSTR / OP_LEFT / OP_RIGHT: operands read and clamped as ts-sdk reads them; ts-sdk's range rules (an
  offset must be inside the data); negative operands no longer wrap to huge indexes and panic.
- A push that runs past the end of its script fails, executed or not (chunks record their declared length).
- An OP_IF left open by the unlocking script fails; the alt stack does not carry into the locking script.

Found by b017-native's interpreter differential (20000 random scripts through ts-sdk Spend).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
